      "rev_n_1_4713f7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_4713f7_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_4713f7",
        "thought_text": "Implement a recursive depth-first search (DFS) function that takes the current array slice, checks if it is sorted, and if not, recursively calculates the maximum sorted length by removing either the first half or the second half, returning the maximum of those two results.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794587447.6771588
      },
      "rev_n_2_a094db_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_a094db_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_a094db",
        "thought_text": "Use memoization to store results for specific array slices to avoid redundant calculations, though given the small constraint $n \\le 16$, a simple recursive solution without explicit memoization might suffice but could be slightly less efficient in worst-case scenarios.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794587447.6771805
      },
      "rev_n_3_1701eb_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_1701eb_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_1701eb",
        "thought_text": "Iteratively simulate the process by maintaining a set of valid subarray ranges and pruning branches where the subarray is not sorted, effectively exploring the decision tree of 'remove left' vs 'remove right' until only sorted subarrays remain, then taking the maximum length.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794587447.6771934
      },
      "rev_n_4_75ccb1_0": {
